e has that on operators in $\\cA_F$\n    embedded in $\\cP_I$ that the local density matrix is\n    \\begin{equation}\\label{eq:tilde_omega}\n        \\tilde{\\w} = \\bigotimes_{i \\in (F \\cap P)} \\ket{0}\\bra{0} \\otimes \\bigotimes_{j\n        \\in F \\setminus P}\n        \\frac{\\id_j}{2}.\n    \\end{equation}\n    This can be inferred by checking it on Pauli-words, which form a basis of\n    the operators. We can write a general Pauli word up to a phase via\n    \\begin{equation}\n       O = \\prod_{i \\in F} (X_i)^{a_i} (Z_i)^{b_i}. \n    \\end{equation}\n    If any $a_i,b_i \\neq 0$ for $i \\notin P$ or $a_j \\neq 0$ for $j \\in P$,\n    then $\\tilde{\\w}(O) = 0$, as the resulting operator in $\\cA_\\G$ will have a\n    string that is \\LS{In the symplectic complement??} of $W_\\G$, which are\n    annihilated. If the conditions of the previous sentence are not satisfied,\n    then the operator $\\a(O)$ will have a Pauli string that is inside $W_\\G$,\n    which has expectation values $1$. One can check that the state\n    \\eqref{eq:tilde_omega} has the same expectation value. Since the states\n    agree on a basis of operators, they are the same states. Thus the state\n    on $\\G$ can be represented by a density matrix which is a tensor product of\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
